<%# THE TROUBLE STRIP — rendered ONLY when something real needs a human (answers-first spec §5,
    computed-claims §4). Local: `presenter` (HomePresenter).

    ** SILENCE IS THE GOOD STATE. ** The strip is absent when nothing is true, and the caller gates on
    `HomePresenter#trouble?` so there is one gate rather than five predicates OR'd in a view.

    ** THE `:undistributed` ARM IS DELETED (computed-claims §6). ** "This period hasn't been
    distributed yet — your rules are asking for money nobody has handed out" was trouble only because
    money had to be MOVED before a category could hold any. Claims are computed from the rules, the
    calendar and the spending (§3), so there is nothing to hand out, nothing to have missed, and no
    Distribute button to put here. What replaces it is the state §4 says IS worth a human: free below
    zero, who gives way, and the pace that lands the period at zero.

    ** THE FIX BUTTONS ARE DELETED WITH IT (the same §5). ** Every problem row used to offer
    "Take $300.00 from Rent" — an ALLOCATION, a purpose-side move — and there are no purpose-side
    moves. A claim cannot be topped up out of another claim, because neither is money sitting
    anywhere; what changes one is a rule, a dated adjustment (§3.3) or spending less. The strip says
    that in words and its one door is the Budget page, which is where both of those are written.

    FOUR KINDS, AND EACH IS A DIFFERENT KIND OF TROUBLE RATHER THAN THE SAME ONE FOUR WAYS. The types
    come off `HomePresenter#troubles`; a `case` on the kind is what keeps a new trigger from being
    added to the list and forgotten in the markup. %>
<section class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded" data-trouble aria-labelledby="trouble-heading">
  <div class="px-6 py-4 border-b border-gray-200">
    <%# The count is the heading, because the count is the news. Sentence case, not the `uppercase`
        treatment this codebase gives short labels: that renders a whole sentence as a shout. %>
    <h3 id="trouble-heading" class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900">
      <%= pluralize(presenter.troubles.size, "thing") %> need<%= "s" if presenter.troubles.one? %> you
    </h3>
  </div>

  <% presenter.troubles.each do |trouble| %>
    <% case trouble.kind %>
    <% when :overdraft %>
      <%# A NON-MAIN ACCOUNT BELOW ZERO — the standing band's strip, with its praised copy verbatim.
          Main is excluded by `HomePresenter#overdrawn_other_accounts`, because main's overdraft IS
          the hero's red "In Checking" figure and its last clause is exactly true of the accounts left
          here.

          NO BUTTON, AND THAT WAS ALREADY THE RULING: an overdrawn bank account is a PHYSICAL fact,
          and nothing on the purpose side can reach it. What went away is the button, not the debt. %>
      <p class="px-6 py-3 border-b border-gray-100 last:border-b-0 bg-status-danger-light text-sm font-medium text-status-danger"
         data-overdrawn-account="<%= trouble.subject.name %>">
        <%= trouble.subject.name %> is overdrawn <%= number_to_currency(presenter.overdraft_for(trouble.subject)) %> —
        already spent, and none of the figures above count it.
      </p>

    <% when :shortfall %>
      <%= render "shortfall", presenter: presenter %>

    <% when :over, :overdue %>
      <%# ONE RULE THAT NEEDS A HUMAN. The line is `HomePresenter::ClaimLine` — the SAME object the
          "This period" row below prints — so the two cannot describe one rule differently, which is
          exactly what happened when the strip read a `HoldingStatus` and the section read a bar.

          The category is the subject and the rule is named only where the category has more than one:
          a single-rule category IS its rule, and printing both would be the same noun twice. %>
      <div class="px-6 py-3 border-b border-gray-100 last:border-b-0 <%= "bg-status-danger-light" if trouble.kind == :over %>"
           data-problem-category="<%= trouble.subject.category.name %>">
        <div class="flex justify-between items-baseline gap-4">
          <span class="font-medium text-gray-900"><%= trouble.subject.category.name %></span>
          <span class="text-sm text-status-danger font-medium" data-problem-state>
            <%= claim_trouble_label(trouble.subject) %>
          </span>
        </div>
        <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-600" data-problem-detail>
          <%= pool_rule_label(trouble.subject.rule) %> ·
          <% if trouble.kind == :over %>
            <%= number_to_currency(trouble.subject.spent) %> spent of <%= number_to_currency(trouble.subject.accrued) %>
            — the excess comes straight out of what is free.
          <% else %>
            <%= number_to_currency(trouble.subject.built_up) %> built up of
            <%= number_to_currency(trouble.subject.target) %> — this needs paying, and the fund goes on
            asking until it is.
          <% end %>
        </p>
      </div>

    <% when :structural %>
      <%# §9'S PERMANENT BUTTON. It is a verdict about the SHAPE of the rules, which no amount of care
          this period can fix — which is exactly why it belongs on a strip about things that need a
          human rather than beside this period's cash.

          `structurally_underwater?` is gated on income AND cadence together and memoised for the
          false case too, so this costs nothing on the screens that do not render it. A recomputation
          here could say "your budget doesn't fit your income" beside a /budget page that says it
          fits — and the route this button opens refuses on exactly that reader. %>
      <div class="px-6 py-3 border-b border-gray-100 last:border-b-0">
        <%= link_to("Your budget doesn't fit your income",
                    sacrifice_path,
                    class: "inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-4 py-2 rounded border " \
                           "border-status-danger text-status-danger text-sm font-medium hover:bg-status-danger-light",
                    data: { sacrifice_link: true }) %>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>
</section>
